        If 'balanced', class weights will be given by\n 15 |         ``n_samples / (n_classes * np.bincount(y))``.\n 16 |         If a dictionary is given, keys are classes and values\n 17 |         are corresponding class weights.\n 18 |         If None is given, the class weights will be uniform.\n 19 | \n 20 |     classes : ndarray\n 21 |         Array of the classes occurring in the data, as given by\n 22 |         ``np.unique(y_org)`` with ``y_org`` the original class labels.\n 23 | \n 24 |     y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n 25 |         Array of original class labels per sample.\n 26 | \n 27 |     Returns\n 28 |     -------\n 29 |     class_weight_vect : ndarray of shape (n_classes,)\n 30 |         Array with class_weight_vect[i] the weight for i-th class.\n 31 | \n 32 |     References\n 33 |     ----------\n 34 |     The \"balanced\" heuristic is inspired by\n 35 |     Logistic Regression in Rare Events Data, King, Zen, 2001.\n 36 |     \"\"\"\n 37 |     # Import error caused by circular imports.\n 38 |     from ..preprocessing import LabelEncoder\n 39 | \n 40 |     if set(y) - set(classes):\n 41 |         raise ValueError(\"classes should include all valid labels that can be in y\")\n```\nHint: \nThe code for function `sklearn/utils/class_weight.py:compute_class_weight` is incomplete; search `sklearn/utils/class_weight.py:compute_class_weight` for the full content if needed.\n\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n840 | def calibration_curve(y_true, y_prob, *, normalize=False, n_bins=5, strategy=\"uniform\"):\n841 |     \"\"\"Compute true and predicted probabilities for a calibration curve.\n842 | \n843 |     The method assumes the inputs come from a binary classifier, and\n844 |     discretize the [0, 1] interval into bins.\n845 | \n846 |     Calibration curves may also be referred to as reliability diagrams.\n847 | \n848 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <calibration>`.\n849 | \n850 |     Parameters\n851 |     ----------\n852 |     y_true : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n853 |         True targets.\n854 | \n855 |     y_prob : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n856 |         Probabilities of the positive class.\n857 | \n858 |     normalize : bool, default=False\n859 |         Whether y_prob needs to be normalized into the [0, 1] interval, i.e.\n860 |         is not a proper probability. If True, the smallest value in y_prob\n861 |         is linearly mapped onto 0 and the largest one onto 1.\n862 | \n863 |     n_bins : int, default=5\n864 |         Number of bins to discretize the [0, 1] interval. A bigger number\n865 |         requires more data. Bins with no samples (i.e. without\n866 |         corresponding values in `y_prob`) will not be returned, thus the\n867 |         returned arrays may have less than `n_bins` values.\n868 | \n869 |     strategy : {'uniform', 'quantile'}, default='uniform'\n870 |         Strategy used to define the widths of the bins.\n871 | \n872 |         uniform\n873 |             The bins have identical widths.\n874 |         quantile\n875 |             The bins have the same number of samples and depend on `y_prob`.\n876 | \n877 |     Returns\n878 |     -------\n879 |     prob_true : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n880 |         The proportion of samples whose class is the positive class, in each\n881 |         bin (fraction of positives).\n882 | \n883 |     prob_pred : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n884 |         The mean predicted probability in each bin.\n885 | \n886 |     References\n887 |     ----------\n888 |     Alexandru Niculescu-Mizil and Rich Caruana (2005) Predicting Good\n889 |     Probabilities With Supervised Learning, in Proceedings of the 22nd\n890 |     International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML).\n891 |     See section 4 (Qualitative Analysis of Predictions).\n892 | \n893 |     Examples\n894 |     --------\n895 |     >>> import numpy as np\n896 |     >>> from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve\n897 |     >>> y_true = np.array([0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1])\n898 |     >>> y_pred = np.array([0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.65, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9,  1.])\n899 |     >>> prob_true, prob_pred = calibration_curve(y_true, y_pred, n_bins=3)\n900 |     >>> prob_true\n901 |     array([0. , 0.5, 1. ])\n902 |     >>> prob_pred\n903 |     array([0.2  , 0.525, 0.85 ])\n904 |     \"\"\"\n905 |     y_true = column_or_1d(y_true)\n906 |     y_prob = column_or_1d(y_prob)\n907 |     check_consistent_length(y_true, y_prob)\n908 | \n909 |     if normalize:  # Normalize predicted values into interval [0, 1]\n910 |         y_prob = (y_prob - y_prob.min()) / (y_prob.max() - y_prob.min())\n911 |     elif y_prob.min() < 0 or y_prob.max() > 1:\n912 |         raise ValueError(\n913 |             \"y_prob has values outside [0, 1] and normalize is set to False.\"\n914 |         )\n915 | \n916 |     labels = np.unique(y_true)\n917 |     if len(labels) > 2:\n918 |         raise ValueError(\n919 |             \"Only binary classification is supported. Provided labels %s.\" % labels\n920 |         )\n921 |     y_true = label_binarize(y_true, classes=labels)[:, 0]\n922 | \n923 |     if strategy == \"quantile\":  # Determine bin edges by distribution of data\n924 |         quantiles = np.linspace(0, 1, n_bins + 1)\n925 |         bins = np.percentile(y_prob, quantiles * 100)\n926 |         bins[-1] = bins[-1] + 1e-8\n927 |     elif strategy == \"uniform\":\n928 |         bins = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0 + 1e-8, n_bins + 1)\n929 |     else:\n930 |         raise ValueError(\n931 |             \"Invalid entry to 'strategy' input. Strategy \"\n932 |             \"must be either 'quantile' or 'uniform'.\"\n933 |         )\n934 | \n935 |     binids = np.digitize(y_prob, bins) - 1\n936 | \n937 |     bin_sums = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_prob, minlength=len(bins))\n938 |     bin_true = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_true, minlength=len(bins))\n939 |     bin_total = np.bincount(binids, minlength=len(bins))\n940 | \n941 |     nonzero = bin_total != 0\n942 |     prob_true = bin_true[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n943 |     prob_pred = bin_sums[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n944 | \n945 |     return prob_true, prob_pred\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/random.py:_random_choice_csc`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n14 | def _random_choice_csc(n_samples, classes, class_probability=None, random_state=None):\n15 |     \"\"\"Generate a sparse random matrix given column class distributions\n16 | \n17 |     Parameters\n18 |     ----------\n19 |     n_samples : int,\n20 |         Number of samples to draw in each column.\n21 | \n22 |     classes : list of size n_outputs of arrays of size (n_classes,)\n23 |         List of classes for each column.\n24 | \n25 |     class_probability : list of size n_outputs of arrays of \\\n26 |         shape (n_classes,), default=None\n27 |         Class distribution of each column. If None, uniform distribution is\n28 |         assumed.\n29 | \n30 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n31 |         Controls the randomness of the sampled classes.\n32 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n33 | \n34 |     Returns\n35 |     -------\n36 |     random_matrix : sparse csc matrix of size (n_samples, n_outputs)\n37 | \n38 |     \"\"\"\n39 |     data = array.array(\"i\")\n40 |     indices = array.array(\"i\")\n41 |     indptr = array.array(\"i\", [0])\n42 | \n43 |     for j in range(len(classes)):\n44 |         classes[j] = np.asarray(classes[j])\n45 |         if classes[j].dtype.kind != \"i\":\n46 |             raise ValueError(\"class dtype %s is not supported\" % classes[j].dtype)\n47 |         classes[j] = classes[j].astype(np.int64, copy=False)\n48 | \n49 |         # use uniform distribution if no class_probability is given\n50 |         if class_probability is None:\n51 |             class_prob_j = np.empty(shape=classes[j].shape[0])\n52 |             class_prob_j.fill(1 / classes[j].shape[0])\n53 |         else:\n54 |             class_prob_j = np.asarray(class_probability[j])\n55 | \n56 |         if not np.isclose(np.sum(class_prob_j), 1.0):\n57 |             raise ValueError(\n58 |                 \"Probability array at index {0} does not sum to one\".format(j)\n59 |             )\n60 | \n61 |         if class_prob_j.shape[0] != classes[j].shape[0]:\n62 |             raise ValueError(\n63 |                 \"classes[{0}] (length {1}) and \"\n64 |                 \"class_probability[{0}] (length {2}) have \"\n65 |                 \"different length.\".format(\n66 |                     j, classes[j].shape[0], class_prob_j.shape[0]\n67 |                 )\n68 |             )\n69 | \n70 |         # If 0 is not present in the classes insert it with a probability 0.0\n71 |         if 0 not in classes[j]:\n72 |             classes[j] = np.insert(classes[j], 0, 0)\n73 |             class_prob_j = np.insert(class_prob_j, 0, 0.0)\n74 | \n75 |         # If there are nonzero classes choose randomly using class_probability\n76 |         rng = check_random_state(random_state)\n77 |         if classes[j].shape[0] > 1:\n78 |             p_nonzero = 1 - class_prob_j[classes[j] == 0]\n79 |             nnz = int(n_samples * p_nonzero)\n80 |             ind_sample = sample_without_replacement(\n81 |                 n_population=n_samples, n_samples=nnz, random_state=random_state\n82 |             )\n83 |             indices.extend(ind_sample)\n84 | \n85 |             # Normalize probabilities for the nonzero elements\n86 |             classes_j_nonzero = classes[j] != 0\n87 |             class_probability_nz = class_prob_j[classes_j_nonzero]\n88 |             class_probability_nz_norm = class_probability_nz / np.sum(\n89 |                 class_probability_nz\n90 |             )\n91 |             classes_ind = np.searchsorted(\n92 |                 class_probability_nz_norm.cumsum(), rng.rand(nnz)\n93 |             )\n94 |             data.extend(classes[j][classes_j_nonzero][classes_ind])\n95 |         indptr.append(len(indices))\n96 | \n97 |     return sp.csc_matrix((data, indices, indptr), (n_samples, len(classes)), dtype=int)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/preprocessing/_label.py:MultiLabelBinarizer.fit`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n742 | def fit(self, y):\n743 |         \"\"\"Fit the label sets binarizer, storing :term:`classes_`.\n744 | \n745 |         Parameters\n746 |         ----------\n747 |         y : iterable of iterables\n748 |             A set of labels (any orderable and hashable object) for each\n749 |             sample. If the `classes` parameter is set, `y` will not be\n750 |             iterated.\n751 | \n752 |         Returns\n753 |         -------\n754 |         self : returns this MultiLabelBinarizer instance\n755 |         \"\"\"\n756 |         self._cached_dict = None\n757 |         if self.classes is None:\n758 |             classes = sorted(set(itertools.chain.from_iterable(y)))\n759 |         elif len(set(self.classes)) < len(self.classes):\n760 |             raise ValueError(\n761 |                 \"The classes argument contains duplicate \"\n762 |                 \"classes. Remove these duplicates before passing \"\n763 |                 \"them to MultiLabelBinarizer.\"\n764 |             )\n765 |         else:\n766 |             classes = self.classes\n767 |         dtype = int if all(isinstance(c, int) for c in classes) else object\n768 |         self.classes_ = np.empty(len(classes), dtype=dtype)\n769 |         self.classes_[:] = classes\n770 |         return self\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/class_weight.py:compute_class_weight`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 8 | def compute_class_weight(class_weight, *, classes, y):\n 9 |     \"\"\"Estimate class weights for unbalanced datasets.\n10 | \n11 |     Parameters\n12 |     ----------\n13 |     class_weight : dict, 'balanced' or None\n14 |         If 'balanced', class weights will be given by\n15 |         ``n_samples / (n_classes * np.bincount(y))``.\n16 |         If a dictionary is given, keys are classes and values\n17 |         are corresponding class weights.\n18 |         If None is given, the class weights will be uniform.\n19 | \n20 |     classes : ndarray\n21 |         Array of the classes occurring in the data, as given by\n22 |         ``np.unique(y_org)`` with ``y_org`` the original class labels.\n23 | \n24 |     y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n25 |         Array of original class labels per sample.\n26 | \n27 |     Returns\n28 |     -------\n29 |     class_weight_vect : ndarray of shape (n_classes,)\n30 |         Array with class_weight_vect[i] the weight for i-th class.\n31 | \n32 |     References\n33 |     ----------\n34 |     The \"balanced\" heuristic is inspired by\n35 |     Logistic Regression in Rare Events Data, King, Zen, 2001.\n36 |     \"\"\"\n37 |     # Import error caused by circular imports.\n38 |     from ..preprocessing import LabelEncoder\n39 | \n40 |     if set(y) - set(classes):\n41 |         raise ValueError(\"classes should include all valid labels that can be in y\")\n42 |     if class_weight is None or len(class_weight) == 0:\n43 |         # uniform class weights\n44 |         weight = np.ones(classes.shape[0], dtype=np.float64, order=\"C\")\n45 |     elif class_weight == \"balanced\":\n46 |         # Find the weight of each class as present in y.\n47 |         le = LabelEncoder()\n48 |         y_ind = le.fit_transform(y)\n49 |         if not all(np.in1d(classes, le.classes_)):\n50 |             raise ValueError(\"classes should have valid labels that are in y\")\n51 | \n52 |         recip_freq = len(y) / (len(le.classes_) * np.bincount(y_ind).astype(np.float64))\n53 |         weight = recip_freq[le.transform(classes)]\n54 |     else:\n55 |         # user-defined dictionary\n56 |         weight = np.ones(classes.shape[0], dtype=np.float64, order=\"C\")\n57 |         if not isinstance(class_weight, dict):\n58 |             raise ValueError(\n59 |                 \"class_weight must be dict, 'balanced', or None, got: %r\" % class_weight\n60 |             )\n61 |         for c in class_weight:\n62 |             i = np.searchsorted(classes, c)\n63 |             if i >= len(classes) or classes[i] != c:\n64 |                 raise ValueError(\"Class label {} not present.\".format(c))\n65 |             else:\n66 |                 weight[i] = class_weight[c]\n67 | \n68 |     return weight\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_scorer.py:_BaseScorer._check_pos_label`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n148 | def _check_pos_label(pos_label, classes):\n149 |         if pos_label not in list(classes):\n150 |             raise ValueError(f\"pos_label={pos_label} is not a valid label: {classes}\")\n```\n\nFound file `sklearn/calibration.py`.\nFound file `examples/calibration/plot_calibration_multiclass.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n"}, {"content": "Let me look at the plotting base class since it seems relevant:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['plot', '_plot_calibration']</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"plot\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/det_curve.py:DetCurveDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py:RocCurveDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py:ConfusionMatrixDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/inspe