keyword search (bm25).\n```\n   1 | \"\"\"\n   2 | The :mod:`sklearn.pls` module implements Partial Least Squares (PLS).\n   3 | \"\"\"\n   4 | \n   5 | # Authors: The scikit-learn developers\n   6 | # SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause\n   7 | \n   8 | import warnings\n   9 | from abc import ABCMeta, abstractmethod\n  10 | from numbers import Integral, Real\n  11 | \n  12 | import numpy as np\n  13 | from scipy.linalg import svd\n  14 | \n  15 | from ..base import (\n  16 |     BaseEstimator,\n  17 |     ClassNamePrefixFeaturesOutMixin,\n  18 |     MultiOutputMixin,\n  19 |     RegressorMixin,\n  20 |     TransformerMixin,\n  21 |     _fit_context,\n  22 | )\n  23 | from ..exceptions import ConvergenceWarning\n  24 | from ..utils import check_array, check_consistent_length\n  25 | from ..utils._param_validation import Interval, StrOptions\n  26 | from ..utils.extmath import svd_flip\n  27 | from ..utils.fixes import parse_version, sp_version\n  28 | from ..utils.validation import FLOAT_DTYPES, check_is_fitted\n  29 | \n  30 | __all__ = [\"PLSCanonical\", \"PLSRegression\", \"PLSSVD\"]\n  31 | \n  32 | \n  33 | if sp_version >= parse_version(\"1.7\"):\n  34 |     # Starting in scipy 1.7 pinv2 was deprecated in favor of pinv.\n  35 |     # pinv now uses the svd to compute the pseudo-inverse.\n  36 |     from scipy.linalg import pinv as pinv2\n  37 | else:\n  38 |     from scipy.linalg import pinv2\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:check_consistent_length`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n451 | def check_consistent_length(*arrays):\n452 |     \"\"\"Check that all arrays have consistent first dimensions.\n453 | \n454 |     Checks whether all objects in arrays have the same shape or length.\n455 | \n456 |     Parameters\n457 |     ----------\n458 |     *arrays : list or tuple of input objects.\n459 |         Objects that will be checked for consistent length.\n460 | \n461 |     Examples\n462 |     --------\n463 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import check_consistent_length\n464 |     >>> a = [1, 2, 3]\n465 |     >>> b = [2, 3, 4]\n466 |     >>> check_consistent_length(a, b)\n467 |     \"\"\"\n468 | \n469 |     lengths = [_num_samples(X) for X in arrays if X is not None]\n470 |     uniques = np.unique(lengths)\n471 |     if len(uniques) > 1:\n472 |         raise ValueError(\n473 |             \"Found input variables with inconsistent numbers of samples: %r\"\n474 |             % [int(l) for l in lengths]\n475 |         )\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:_to_object_array`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2579 | def _to_object_array(sequence):\n2580 |     \"\"\"Convert sequence to a 1-D NumPy array of object dtype.\n2581 | \n2582 |     numpy.array constructor has a similar use but it's output\n2583 |     is ambiguous. It can be 1-D NumPy array of object dtype if\n2584 |     the input is a ragged array, but if the input is a list of\n2585 |     equal length arrays, then the output is a 2D numpy.array.\n2586 |     _to_object_array solves this ambiguity by guarantying that\n2587 |     the output is a 1-D NumPy array of objects for any input.\n2588 | \n2589 |     Parameters\n2590 |     ----------\n2591 |     sequence : array-like of shape (n_elements,)\n2592 |         The sequence to be converted.\n2593 | \n2594 |     Returns\n2595 |     -------\n2596 |     out : ndarray of shape (n_elements,), dtype=object\n2597 |         The converted sequence into a 1-D NumPy array of object dtype.\n2598 | \n2599 |     Examples\n2600 |     --------\n2601 |     >>> import numpy as np\n2602 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import _to_object_array\n2603 |     >>> _to_object_array([np.array([0]), np.array([1])])\n2604 |     array([array([0]), array([1])], dtype=object)\n2605 |     >>> _to_object_array([np.array([0]), np.array([1, 2])])\n2606 |     array([array([0]), array([1, 2])], dtype=object)\n2607 |     >>> _to_object_array([np.array([0]), np.array([1, 2])])\n2608 |     array([array([0]), array([1, 2])], dtype=object)\n2609 |     \"\"\"\n2610 |     out = np.empty(len(sequence), dtype=object)\n2611 |     out[:] = sequence\n2612 |     return out\n```\n\nFound function `doc/conftest.py:setup_compose`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n58 | def setup_compose():\n59 |     try:\n60 |         import pandas  # noqa\n61 |     except ImportError:\n62 |         raise SkipTest(\"Skipping compose.rst, pandas not installed\")\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:_pinv2_old`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n41 | def _pinv2_old(a):\n42 |     # Used previous scipy pinv2 that was updated in:\n43 |     # https://github.com/scipy/scipy/pull/10067\n44 |     # We can not set `cond` or `rcond` for pinv2 in scipy >= 1.3 to keep the\n45 |     # same behavior of pinv2 for scipy < 1.3, because the condition used to\n46 |     # determine the rank is dependent on the output of svd.\n47 |     u, s, vh = svd(a, full_matrices=False, check_finite=False)\n48 | \n49 |     t = u.dtype.char.lower()\n50 |     factor = {\"f\": 1e3, \"d\": 1e6}\n51 |     cond = np.max(s) * factor[t] * np.finfo(t).eps\n52 |     rank = np.sum(s > cond)\n53 | \n54 |     u = u[:, :rank]\n55 |     u /= s[:rank]\n56 |     return np.transpose(np.conjugate(np.dot(u, vh[:rank])))\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:_get_first_singular_vectors_power_method`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 59 | def _get_first_singular_vectors_power_method(\n 60 |     X, Y, mode=\"A\", max_iter=500, tol=1e-06, norm_y_weights=False\n 61 | ):\n 62 |     \"\"\"Return the first left and right singular vectors of X'Y.\n 63 | \n 64 |     Provides an alternative to the svd(X'Y) and uses the power method instead.\n 65 |     With norm_y_weights to True and in mode A, this corresponds to the\n 66 |     algorithm section 11.3 of the Wegelin's review, except this starts at the\n 67 |     \"update saliences\" part.\n 68 |     \"\"\"\n 69 | \n 70 |     eps = np.finfo(X.dtype).eps\n 71 |     try:\n 72 |         y_score = next(col for col in Y.T if np.any(np.abs(col) > eps))\n 73 |     except StopIteration as e:\n 74 |         raise StopIteration(\"y residual is constant\") from e\n 75 | \n 76 |     x_weights_old = 100  # init to big value for first convergence check\n 77 | \n 78 |     if mode == \"B\":\n 79 |         # Precompute pseudo inverse matrices\n 80 |         # Basically: X_pinv = (X.T X)^-1 X.T\n 81 |         # Which requires inverting a (n_features, n_features) matrix.\n 82 |         # As a result, and as detailed in the Wegelin's review, CCA (i.e. mode\n 83 |         # B) will be unstable if n_features > n_samples or n_targets >\n 84 |         # n_samples\n 85 |         X_pinv, Y_pinv = _pinv2_old(X), _pinv2_old(Y)\n 86 | \n 87 |     for i in range(max_iter):\n 88 |         if mode == \"B\":\n 89 |             x_weights = np.dot(X_pinv, y_score)\n 90 |         else:\n 91 |             x_weights = np.dot(X.T, y_score) / np.dot(y_score, y_score)\n 92 | \n 93 |         x_weights /= np.sqrt(np.dot(x_weights, x_weights)) + eps\n 94 |         x_score = np.dot(X, x_weights)\n 95 | \n 96 |         if mode == \"B\":\n 97 |             y_weights = np.dot(Y_pinv, x_score)\n 98 |         else:\n 99 |             y_weights = np.dot(Y.T, x_score) / np.dot(x_score.T, x_score)\n100 | \n101 |         if norm_y_weights:\n102 |             y_weights /= np.sqrt(np.dot(y_weights, y_weights)) + eps\n103 | \n104 |         y_score = np.dot(Y, y_weights) / (np.dot(y_weights, y_weights) + eps)\n105 | \n106 |         x_weights_diff = x_weights - x_weights_old\n107 |         if np.dot(x_weights_diff, x_weights_diff) < tol or Y.shape[1] == 1:\n108 |             break\n109 |         x_weights_old = x_weights\n110 | \n111 |     n_iter = i + 1\n112 |     if n_iter == max_iter:\n113 |         warnings.warn(\"Maximum number of iterations reached\", ConvergenceWarning)\n114 | \n115 |     return x_weights, y_weights, n_iter\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:_center_scale_xy`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n128 | def _center_scale_xy(X, Y, scale=True):\n129 |     \"\"\"Center X, Y and scale if the scale parameter==True\n130 | \n131 |     Returns\n132 |     -------\n133 |         X, Y, x_mean, y_mean, x_std, y_std\n134 |     \"\"\"\n135 |     # center\n136 |     x_mean = X.mean(axis=0)\n137 |     X -= x_mean\n138 |     y_mean = Y.mean(axis=0)\n139 |     Y -= y_mean\n140 |     # scale\n141 |     if scale:\n142 |         x_std = X.std(axis=0, ddof=1)\n143 |         x_std[x_std == 0.0] = 1.0\n144 |         X /= x_std\n145 |         y_std = Y.std(axis=0, ddof=1)\n146 |         y_std[y_std == 0.0] = 1.0\n147 |         Y /= y_std\n148 |     else:\n149 |         x_std = np.ones(X.shape[1])\n150 |         y_std = np.ones(Y.shape[1])\n151 |     return X, Y, x_mean, y_mean, x_std, y_std\n```\n\nFound file `doc/conftest.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Search `doc/conftest.py` for the full content if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"docstring consistency\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `examples/inspection/plot_permutation_importance_multicollinear.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | =================================================================\n  3 | Permutation Importance with Multicollinear or Correlated Features\n  4 | =================================================================\n  5 | \n  6 | In this example, we compute the\n  7 | :func:`~sklearn.inspection.permutation_importance` of the features to a trained\n  8 | :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestClassifier` using the\n  9 | :ref:`breast_cancer_dataset`. The model can easily get about 97% accuracy on a\n 10 | test dataset. Because this dataset contains multicollinear features, the\n 11 | permutation importance shows that none of the features are important, in\n 12 | contradiction with the high test accuracy.\n 13 | \n 14 | We demo a possible approach to handling multicollinearity, which consists of\n 15 | hierarchical clustering on the features' Spearman rank-order correlations,\n 16 | picking a threshold, and keeping a single feature from each cluster.\n 17 | \n 18 | .. note::\n 19 |     See also\n 20 |     :ref:`sphx_glr_auto_examples_inspection_plot_permutation_importance.py`\n 21 | \n 22 | \"\"\"\n 23 | \n 24 | # Authors: The scikit-learn developers\n 25 | # SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause\n 26 | \n 27 | # %%\n 28 | # Random Forest Feature Importance on Breast Cancer Data\n 29 | # ------------------------------------------------------\n 30 | #\n 31 | # First, we define a function to ease the plotting:\n 32 | import matplotlib\n 33 | \n 34 | from sklearn.inspection import permutation_importance\n 35 | from sklearn.utils.fixes import parse_version\n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `examples/decomposition/plot_faces_decomposition.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 25 | import logging\n 26 | \n 27 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 28 | from numpy.random import RandomState\n 29 | \n 30 | from sklearn import cluster, decomposition\n 31 | from sklearn.datasets import fetch_olivetti_faces\n 32 | \n 33 | rng = RandomState(0)\n 34 | \n 35 | # Display progress logs on stdout\n 36 | logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=\"%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(message)s\")\n 37 | \n 38 | faces, _ = fetch_olivetti_faces(return_X_y=True, shuffle=True, random_state=rng)\n 39 | n_samples, n_features = faces.shape\n 40 | \n 41 | # Global centering (focus on one feature, centering all samples)\n 42 | faces_centered = faces - faces.mean(axis=0)\n 43 | \n 44 | # Local centering (focus on one sample, centering all features)\n 45 | faces_centered -= faces_centered.mean(axis=1).reshape(n_samples, -1)\n 46 | \n 47 | print(\"Dataset consists of %d faces\" % n_samples)\n 48 | \n 49 | # %%\n 50 | # Define a base function to plot the gallery of faces.\n 51 | \n 52 | n_row, n_col = 2, 3\n 53 | n_components = n_row * n_col\n 54 | image_shape = (64, 64)\n```\n\nFound `sklearn/utils/_indexing.py:_array_indexing`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  4 | import numbers\n  5 | import sys\n  6 | import warnings\n  7 | from collections import UserList\n  8 | from itertools import compress, islice\n  9 | \n 10 | import numpy as np\n 11 | from scipy.sparse import issparse\n 12 | \n 13 | from ._array_api import _is_numpy_namespace, get_namespace\n 14 | from ._param_validation import Interval, validate_params\n 15 | from .extmath import _approximate_mode\n 16 | from .validation import (\n 17 |     _is_arraylike_not_scalar,\n 18 |     _is_pandas_df,\n 19 |     _is_polars_df_or_series,\n 20 |     _use_interchange_protocol,\n 21 |     check_array,\n 22 |     check_consistent_length,\n 23 |     check_random_state,\n 24 | )\n 25 | \n 26 | \n 27 | def _array_indexing(array, key, key_dtype, axis):\n 28 |     \"\"\"Index an array or scipy.sparse consistently across NumPy version.\"\"\"\n 29 |     xp, is_array_api = get_namespace(array)\n 30 |     if is_array_api:\n 31 |         return xp.take(array, key, axis=axis)\n 32 |     if issparse(array) and key_dtype == \"bool\":\n 33 |         key = np.asarray(key)\n 34 |     if isinstance(key, tuple):\n 35 |         key = list(key)\n 36 |     return array[key, ...] if axis == 0 else array[:, key]\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/feature_extraction/text.py:_VectorizerMixin._check_stop_words_consistency`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n374 | def _check_stop_words_consistency(self, stop_words, preprocess, tokenize):\n375 |         \"\"\"Check if stop words are consistent\n376 | \n377 |         Returns\n378 |         -------\n379 |         is_consistent : True if stop words are consistent with the preprocessor\n380 |                         and tokenizer, False if they are not, None if the check\n381 |                         was previously performed, \"error\" if it could not be\n382 |                         performed (e.g. because of the use of a custom\n383 |                         preprocessor / tokenizer)\n384 |         \"\"\"\n385 |         if id(self.stop_words) == getattr(self, \"_stop_words_id\", None):\n386 |             # Stop words are were previously validated\n387 |             return None\n388 | \n389 |         # NB: stop_words is validated, unlike self.stop_words\n390 |         try:\n391 |             inconsistent = set()\n392 |             for w in stop_words or ():\n393 |                 tokens = list(tokenize(preprocess(w)))\n394 |                 for token in tokens:\n395 |                     if token not in stop_words:\n396 |                         inconsistent.add(token)\n397 |             self._stop_words_id = id(self.stop_words)\n398 | \n399 |             if inconsistent:\n400 |                 warnings.warn(\n401 |                     \"Your stop_words may be inconsistent with \"\n402 |                     \"your preprocessing. Tokenizing the stop \"\n403 |                     \"words generated tokens %r not in \"\n404 |                     \"stop_words.\" % sorted(inconsistent)\n405 |                 )\n406 |             return not inconsistent\n407 |         except Exception:\n408 |             # Failed to check stop words consistency (e.g. because a custom\n409 |             # preprocessor or tokenizer was used)\n410 |             self._stop_words_id = id(self.stop_words)\n411 |             return \"error\"\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py:check_pipeline_consistency`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1858 | def check_pipeline_consistency(name, estimator_orig):\n1859 |     if _safe_tags(estimator_orig, key=\"non_deterministic\"):\n1860 |         msg = name + \" is non deterministic\"\n1861 |         raise SkipTest(msg)\n1862 | \n1863 |     # check that make_pipeline(est) gives same score as est\n1864 |     X, y = make_blobs(\n1865 |         n_samples=30,\n1866 |         centers=[[0, 0, 0], [1, 1, 1]],\n1867 |         random_state=0,\n1868 |         n_features=2,\n1869 |         cluster_std=0.1,\n1870 |     )\n1871 |     X = _enforce_estimator_tags_X(estimator_orig, X, kernel=rbf_kernel)\n1872 |     estimator = clone(estimator_orig)\n1873 |     y = _enforce_estimator_tags_y(estimator, y)\n1874 |     set_random_state(estimator)\n1875 |     pipeline = make_pipeline(estimator)\n1876 |     estimator.fit(X, y)\n1877 |     pipeline.fit(X, y)\n1878 | \n1879 |     funcs = [\"score\", \"fit_transform\"]\n1880 | \n1881 |     for func_name in funcs:\n1882 |         func = getattr(estimator, func_name, None)\n1883 |         if func is not None:\n1884 |             func_pipeline = getattr(pipeline, func_name)\n1885 |             result = func(X, y)\n1886 |             result_pipe = func_pipeline(X, y)\n1887 |             assert_allclose_dense_sparse(result, result_pipe)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py:check_decision_proba_consistency`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n3733 | def check_decision_proba_consistency(name, estimator_orig):\n3734 |     # Check whether an estimator having both decision_function and\n3735 |     # predict_proba methods has outputs with perfect rank correlation.\n3736 | \n3737 |     centers = [(2, 2), (4, 4)]\n3738 |     X, y = make_blobs(\n3739 |         n_samples=100,\n3740 |         random_state=0,\n3741 |         n_features=4,\n3742 |         centers=centers,\n3743 |         cluster_std=1.0,\n3744 |         shuffle=True,\n3745 |     )\n3746 |     X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n3747 |         X, y, test_size=0.2, random_state=0\n3748 |     )\n3749 |     estimator = clone(estimator_orig)\n3750 | \n3751 |     if hasattr(estimator, \"decision_function\") and hasattr(estimator, \"predict_proba\"):\n3752 |         estimator.fit(X_train, y_train)\n3753 |         # Since the link function from decision_function() to predict_proba()\n3754 |         # is sometimes not precise enough (typically expit), we round to the\n3755 |         # 10th decimal to avoid numerical issues: we compare the rank\n3756 |         # with deterministic ties rather than get platform specific rank\n3757 |         # inversions in case of machine level differences.\n3758 |         a = estimator.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1].round(decimals=10)\n3759 |         b = estimator.decision_function(X_test).round(decimals=10)\n3760 | \n3761 |         rank_proba, rank_score = rankdata(a), rankdata(b)\n3762 |         try:\n3763 |             assert_array_almost_equal(rank_proba, rank_score)\n3764 |         except AssertionError:\n3765 |             # Sometimes, the rounding applied on the probabilities will have\n3766 |             # ties that are not present in the scores because it is\n3767 |             # numerically more precise. In this case, we relax the test by\n3768 |             # grouping the decision function scores based on the probability\n3769 |             # rank and check that the score is monotonically increasing.\n3770 |             grouped_y_score = np.array(\n3771 |                 [b[rank_proba == group].mean() for group in np.unique(rank_proba)]\n3772 |             )\n3773 |             sorted_idx = np.argsort(grouped_y_score)\n3774 |             assert_array_equal(sorted_idx, np.arange(len(sorted_idx)))\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"docstring consistency\", \"assert_consistent_docs docstring consistency\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `doc/api_reference.py:_get_submodule`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n21 | def _get_submodule(module_name, submodule_name):\n22 |     \"\"\"Get the submodule docstring and automatically add the hook.\n23 | \n24 |     `module_name` is e.g. `sklearn.feature_extraction`, and `submodule_name` is e.g.\n25 |     `image`, so we get the docstring and hook for `sklearn.feature_extraction.image`\n26 |     submodule. `module_name` is used to reset the current module because autosummary\n27 |     automatically changes the current module.\n28 |     \"\"\"\n29 |     lines = [\n30 |         f\".. automodule:: {module_name}.{submodule_name}\",\n31 |         f\".. currentmodule:: {module_name}\",\n32 |     ]\n33 |     return \"\\n\\n\".join(lines)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/feature_extraction/_hash.py:FeatureHasher.fit`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n128 | def fit(self, X=None, y=None):\n129 |         \"\"\"Only validates estimator's parameters.\n130 | \n131 |         This method allows to: (i) validate the estimator's parameters and\n132 |         (ii) be consistent with the scikit-learn transformer API.\n133 | \n134 |         Parameters\n135 |         ----------\n136 |         X : Ignored\n137 |             Not used, present here for API consistency by convention.\n138 | \n139 |         y : Ignored\n140 |             Not used, present here for API consistency by convention.\n141 | \n142 |         Returns\n143 |         -------\n144 |         self : object\n145 |             FeatureHasher class instance.\n146 |         \"\"\"\n147 |         return self\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"assert_consistent_docs docstring consistency\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `examples/inspection/plot_permutation_importance_multicollinear.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | =================================================================\n  3 | Permutation Importance with Multicollinear or Correlated Features\n  4 | =================================================================\n  5 | \n  6 | In this example, we compute the\n  7 | :func:`~sklearn.inspection.permutation_importance` of the features to a trained\n  8 